# Start Here: One Expense Through Jido

## Learning Path

This Livebook connects the six `00_start_here` lessons. It uses the source files
and public APIs from the examples. It does not copy their Agent, Action, Flow,
or Plugin definitions.

Run each cell in order. The notebook uses the current Jido checkout. The example
code makes no network call and needs no credential. Mix can download missing
dependencies when it starts the notebook.

The six lessons use separate modules so that each lesson can run by itself. The
expense domain connects them. Each lesson adds one Jido boundary:

| Lesson  | New boundary                                                        |
| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `00_01` | An Agent is a validated value.                                      |
| `00_02` | An Action validates one unit of work.                               |
| `00_03` | A Flow connects work through data.                                  |
| `00_04` | A Signal selects Agent behavior.                                    |
| `00_05` | AgentServer commits state.                                          |
| `00_06` | A durable host can wait, resume, run an effect, and report failure. |

## Install The Current Checkout

This notebook is inside `examples/00_start_here`. The path points two levels up
to the Jido Mix project.

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)}
])
```

## Load The Example Modules

The notebook loads each existing source file. A reload does not redefine a
module that is already in the Livebook runtime.

```elixir
example_sources = [
  {Jido.Examples.StartHere.ValidatedExpense,
   "00_01_validated_state/validated_state.ex"},
  {Jido.Examples.StartHere.SubmitExpense,
   "00_02_submit_action/submit_action.ex"},
  {Jido.Examples.StartHere.TriageExpense,
   "00_03_triage_flow/triage_flow.ex"},
  {Jido.Examples.StartHere.RoutedExpense,
   "00_04_routed_agent/routed_agent.ex"},
  {Jido.Examples.StartHere.HostedExpense,
   "00_05_hosted_agent/hosted_agent.ex"},
  {Jido.Examples.StartHere.ExpenseNotification,
   "00_06_runtime_inspection/notification.ex"},
  {Jido.Examples.StartHere.ObservedExpense,
   "00_06_runtime_inspection/runtime_inspection.ex"}
]

Enum.each(example_sources, fn {module, relative_path} ->
  unless Code.ensure_loaded?(module) do
    Code.require_file(Path.join(__DIR__, relative_path))
  end
end)

alias Jido.AgentServer

alias Jido.Examples.StartHere.{
  ExpenseNotifications,
  HostedExpense,
  ObservedExpense,
  RoutedExpense,
  SubmitExpense,
  TriageExpense,
  ValidatedExpense
}

:ok
```

## 00_01: Start With Validated State

An Agent starts as an immutable value. Its schema supplies defaults and rejects
invalid state before an Agent value exists. No process starts in this cell.

Files: [lesson](00_01_validated_state/README.md),
[source](00_01_validated_state/validated_state.ex), and
[test](00_01_validated_state/validated_state_test.exs).

```elixir
draft_expense = ValidatedExpense.instantiate!(id: "expense-livebook-draft")

submitted_expense =
  ValidatedExpense.instantiate!(
    id: "expense-livebook-submitted",
    state: %{report_id: "report-7", amount_cents: 12_500, status: :submitted}
  )

invalid_expense =
  ValidatedExpense.instantiate(
    id: "expense-livebook-invalid",
    state: %{report_id: "report-8", amount_cents: -1, status: :submitted}
  )

%{
  default_state: draft_expense.state,
  explicit_state: submitted_expense.state,
  invalid_state_rejected?: match?({:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{}}, invalid_expense)
}
```

The important result is not only the error. The invalid Agent value was never
created.

## 00_02: Run One Action

An Action validates one request and one result. `Jido.Exec` runs the Action
through the same execution model that Jido uses for a one-step Flow.

Files: [lesson](00_02_submit_action/README.md),
[source](00_02_submit_action/submit_action.ex), and
[test](00_02_submit_action/submit_action_test.exs).

```elixir
valid_submission =
  Jido.Exec.run(SubmitExpense, %{report_id: "report-7", amount_cents: 12_500})

invalid_submission =
  Jido.Exec.run(SubmitExpense, %{report_id: "", amount_cents: 0})

%{
  valid: valid_submission,
  invalid_input_rejected?:
    match?({:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{}}, invalid_submission)
}
```

This Action returns a value. It does not store Agent state.

## 00_03: Connect Work In A Flow

The Flow first submits the expense. It then classifies the review from the
submitted result. The explicit output selects the final result.

Files: [lesson](00_03_triage_flow/README.md),
[source](00_03_triage_flow/triage_flow.ex), and
[test](00_03_triage_flow/triage_flow_test.exs).

```elixir
{:ok, automatic_expense} =
  Jido.Exec.run(TriageExpense, %{report_id: "report-7", amount_cents: 12_500})

{:ok, manual_expense} =
  Jido.Exec.run(TriageExpense, %{report_id: "report-8", amount_cents: 75_000})

%{
  small_expense: automatic_expense,
  large_expense: manual_expense
}
```

The Flow is still pure execution. It has no Agent process and makes no commit.

## 00_04: Route A Signal To The Flow

The Agent route maps one Signal type to the triage Flow. `Jido.Agent.cmd/3`
evaluates one Turn. It returns a new candidate Agent and leaves the source
Agent unchanged.

Files: [lesson](00_04_routed_agent/README.md),
[source](00_04_routed_agent/routed_agent.ex), and
[test](00_04_routed_agent/routed_agent_test.exs).

```elixir
routed_source = RoutedExpense.instantiate!(id: "expense-livebook-routed")

{:ok, routed_signal} =
  RoutedExpense.submit_signal(%{report_id: "report-7", amount_cents: 12_500})

{:ok, routed_candidate, routed_directives} =
  Jido.Agent.cmd(routed_source, routed_signal)

%{
  source_status: routed_source.state.status,
  candidate_state: routed_candidate.state,
  directives: routed_directives,
  source_and_candidate_are_different?: routed_source != routed_candidate
}
```

Pure evaluation does not commit the candidate and does not run Directives.

## 00_05: Host And Commit The Same Turn

AgentServer owns one running Agent. It serializes Turns, validates candidate
state, and commits a valid result. The pure and hosted paths use the same Agent
behavior.

Files: [lesson](00_05_hosted_agent/README.md),
[source](00_05_hosted_agent/hosted_agent.ex), and
[test](00_05_hosted_agent/hosted_agent_test.exs).

This cell stops its prior Jido instance before it starts a new one. You can run
the cell more than once.

```elixir
hosted_instance = StartHereLivebook.HostedJido
:ok = Jido.stop(hosted_instance)
{:ok, _hosted_jido} = Jido.start(name: hosted_instance)

hosted_source = HostedExpense.instantiate!(id: "expense-livebook-hosted")

{:ok, hosted_signal} =
  HostedExpense.submit_signal(%{report_id: "report-7", amount_cents: 12_500})

{:ok, pure_candidate, []} = Jido.Agent.cmd(hosted_source, hosted_signal)

{:ok, hosted_server} = Jido.start_agent(hosted_instance, hosted_source)
:ok = AgentServer.await_ready(hosted_server)

{:ok, committed_turn} = AgentServer.call(hosted_server, hosted_signal)
hosted_snapshot = AgentServer.snapshot(hosted_server)

{:ok, invalid_hosted_signal} =
  HostedExpense.submit_signal(%{report_id: "", amount_cents: 0})

{:ok, failed_turn} = AgentServer.call(hosted_server, invalid_hosted_signal)
snapshot_after_failure = AgentServer.snapshot(hosted_server)

%{
  committed_turn_status: committed_turn.status,
  agent_revision: hosted_snapshot.agent_revision,
  pure_and_hosted_state_match?: hosted_snapshot.agent.state == pure_candidate.state,
  failed_turn_status: failed_turn.status,
  failure_changed_committed_state?: snapshot_after_failure != hosted_snapshot
}
```

The last value must be `false`. A failed Turn does not change committed state.

## 00_06: Wait, Resume, Commit, And Run An Effect

This final lesson uses a persistent Jido instance because a Flow wait needs a
durable execution host. The ETS store is local to this Livebook runtime. It
does not survive loss of the BEAM.

The first Signal starts a Turn and leaves it waiting. The approval Signal
resumes the same Turn. AgentServer commits the approved state before the
notification Plugin records its effect.

Files: [lesson](00_06_runtime_inspection/README.md),
[Agent and Flow](00_06_runtime_inspection/runtime_inspection.ex),
[notification capability](00_06_runtime_inspection/notification.ex), and
[test](00_06_runtime_inspection/runtime_inspection_test.exs).

```elixir
runtime_instance = StartHereLivebook.RuntimeJido
:ok = Jido.stop(runtime_instance)

{:ok, _runtime_jido} =
  Jido.start(
    name: runtime_instance,
    namespace: "examples/start-here/livebook",
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: StartHereLivebook.RuntimeStore}
  )

{:ok, runtime_server} =
  Jido.start_agent(runtime_instance, ObservedExpense,
    id: "expense-livebook-observed",
    persistence: :inherit
  )

:ok = AgentServer.await_ready(runtime_server)

{:ok, review_signal} =
  ObservedExpense.review_signal(%{report_id: "report-7", amount_cents: 12_500})

{:ok, waiting_turn} = AgentServer.call(runtime_server, review_signal)
waiting_status = AgentServer.status(runtime_server)

{:ok, approval_signal} = ObservedExpense.decision_signal("report-7", :approve)
{:ok, committed_approval} = AgentServer.call(runtime_server, approval_signal)

{:ok, [notification]} =
  ExpenseNotifications.await(runtime_instance, runtime_server, 1)

{:ok, invalid_review_signal} =
  ObservedExpense.review_signal(%{report_id: "", amount_cents: 0})

{:ok, failed_review} = AgentServer.call(runtime_server, invalid_review_signal)
status_after_failure = AgentServer.status(runtime_server)
snapshot_after_runtime_failure = AgentServer.snapshot(runtime_server)

notification_runtime =
  ExpenseNotifications.runtime(runtime_instance, runtime_server)

notification_monitor = Process.monitor(notification_runtime)
{:ok, stopped_snapshot} = AgentServer.stop(runtime_server)

notification_runtime_stopped? =
  receive do
    {:DOWN, ^notification_monitor, :process, ^notification_runtime, _reason} -> true
  after
    1_000 -> false
  end

%{
  wait: %{
    turn_status: waiting_turn.status,
    work_state: waiting_status.work_state,
    turn_id: waiting_turn.turn_id,
    active_turn_id: waiting_status.active_turn_id
  },
  resume: %{
    turn_status: committed_approval.status,
    same_turn?: committed_approval.turn_id == waiting_turn.turn_id
  },
  effect: %{
    notification_status: notification.status,
    observed_committed_status: notification.snapshot.agent.state.status
  },
  later_failure: %{
    turn_status: failed_review.status,
    committed?: status_after_failure.last_outcome.committed?,
    preserved_agent_status: snapshot_after_runtime_failure.agent.state.status
  },
  cleanup: %{
    stopped_agent_status: stopped_snapshot.agent.state.status,
    notification_runtime_stopped?: notification_runtime_stopped?
  }
}
```

This cell shows three different views:

* `TurnResult` reports the result of one request.
* `Status` reports current operational state.
* `Snapshot` contains the full private AgentServer state.

Build an application-owned public view before you send runtime information to
an external client.

## Clean Up

Stop both Jido instances. This stops the remaining hosted Agent and all owned
processes.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop(hosted_instance)
:ok = Jido.stop(runtime_instance)

%{
  hosted_instance_stopped?: Process.whereis(hosted_instance) == nil,
  runtime_instance_stopped?: Process.whereis(runtime_instance) == nil
}
```

## Check The Chapter

The Livebook is an interactive path. The colocated ExUnit tests remain the
integration proof. From the Jido Mix project, run:

```text
mix test.examples examples/00_start_here --warnings-as-errors
```

Continue with the focused chapters when you need more detail:

* `01_actions` for Action contracts.
* `02_flow_authoring` and `03_flow_execution` for Flows.
* `04_agents` for pure Agent behavior.
* `05_plugins` for reusable capabilities and effects.
* `06_agent_server` for the live runtime.
* `07_instance_catalog_documents` for Jido instances and stable Agent Addresses.
